2026-08-16T12:02:52Z    [HAS:Net fees] GATE critic: You apply a “post‑guidance signal tilt” of −1.31% to place FY2026 net fees “below the guidance midpoint,” but Hays does not publish a numeric Group net‑fees guidance range/midpoint for FY2026—only qualitative trading commentary (and, at times, profit guidance). Without a stated net‑fees guidance midpoint to anchor to, this haircut is on the wrong basis and mechanically depresses the forecast without a valid, checkable anchor. This can be verified against Hays’ FY26 trading updates/RNS, which do not include a net‑fees guidance range.
2026-08-16T12:03:29Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional basic EPS] GATE critic: The 1.31p prior-year base you used is not a full‑year FY2025 figure; it comes from an interim comparison (1.31p vs 4.03p, −67%), i.e., H1 FY2025 vs H1 FY2024. Using an H1 EPS as the baseline to forecast FY2026 full‑year pre‑exceptional basic EPS is a wrong period basis and will bias the result. You should anchor to FY2025 full‑year pre‑exceptional basic EPS (or a like‑for‑like full‑year figure), not an H1 value.
2026-08-16T12:03:55Z    [HAS:Pre-exceptional operating profit] GATE critic: The £45.2m “guidance_anchor” you used appears to be a half‑year (H1 FY26) pre‑exceptional operating profit consensus, not a full‑year FY2026 figure. Your own source for FY2025 shows £45.6m (H1) versus £105.1m (full year), which matches the scale of £45.2m as an H1 number. Anchoring a full‑year FY2026 forecast to an H1 consensus mixes periods and likely understates the full‑year result.
